Hi there, I'm just starting my day today. That's my routine when the world starts resetting after a long sleepaway trip, and until then all I feel is sunny and light. But even here at home, usually means the opposite of that mentality often . I had so many things waiting for me outside but now I find myself surrounded by cute little flower pots, tomato plants, and moss-covered rock walls — which are all relaxing tempers with funny decorations underneath. In some ways, it feels like a revolution against everything familiar. It's like trying to blend into chaos with something so tranquil and peaceful. And how does it end up being something that looks minimal, yet super contrasted? It's like inviting the sun and watching it mix in with the shadows. The joy of letting go, yes.

You know, actually, I think there's something about morning that deserves its own space. Even though it's so fleeting, it holds so much truth. There's a sort of perfection in keeping it simple because sometimes you're looking for permission from others to ease up. Yeah, you know what I mean — maybe when the horizon feels important, or when the idea of breaking down seems like it could be dangerous. Just something that comes up naturally when life gets too harsh. But even after such moments, the reality remains. At least your room can help you try different ideas without feeling overwhelmed.

I’ve also been thinking about how ahead we're going and the upcoming plans. Every year, we come up with more ambitious goals than before, trying to push our limits in every direction. While it's fun to set those high standards, sometimes it's nice to relax and allow things to happen in their natural flow. For instance, a few years ago I tried to make things fit perfectly, but then realized that most of the details didn't matter anymore. Maybe that was one of the true lessons from those years. Maybe everything isn't about fitting together perfectly — sometimes just showing up enough to feel seen and understood is enough. Which makes sense, right? When something doesn't have to feel complete or in order, it's easier to let go and enjoy the process.

In the end, it all falls back to the simple pleasure of having a room where you can settle down and focus on what matters most. That's the kind of place that stays put even during uncertain times. So even if it's nothing special to look at, that still makes it worth living in, isn't it? Because at least you get to chase your dreams without coming across as too bossy or bold. Or perhaps you just have to accept that you're okay with being quiet and calm, as long as you're not forcing yourself to change. Sometimes, the hardest part isn't the pressure — it's learning to accept that some parts of yourself are meant to stay just as they are.

And that's how it's always been, right? Life is about the journey rather than the destination, and each step along the way should feel meaningful and aligned with who you are. Trying to force things to match perfect expectations might just leave you feeling empty instead of filled. No amount of effort or planning will ever truly replace the simple joy of just sitting down and being happy. That's something that feels limitless, I think. At least with my room, I never have to worry about clutter or distractions — I can just breathe and watch the sunshine pour in. Yeah, that sounds like my mission statement. I wish I could paint that tomorrow, though. Hmm...

P.S. By the way, I've got a really good view out the window, but mostly it's foggy from the nearby mountain. Maybe it'll improve once I start seeing clearer details in the distance.

Okay, I guess that's it. Let's take a deep breath and see what the next steps are for our journey. I hope to keep this blog going with energy and purpose, and I'm eager to read anything that might inspire me.

